      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "president-knezhinisky",
      "term": "President Knezhinisky",
      "aliases": [
        "Knezhinisky",
        "Sapormat 'Sport' Knezhinisky",
        "Uncle Sport"
      ],
      "caseSensitive": true,
      "short": "Sapormat 'Sport' Knezhinisky, long-ruling president of the communist People's Republic of Samara, famous for his fitness obsession.",
      "details": "Encyclopedia says he is well into his third decade in power and known abroad for three things: brutal repression of dissent, monumental corruption and a near-pathological obsession with physical fitness. Like all Samaran men over forty, he wears a spectacular walrus moustache.\n\nThe magazine La Fumée reports that 'Uncle Sport', as his people call him, has made bear wrestling the national sport as part of a public fitness drive. Steban and his reading group insist he absorbs a book's content just by staring at its cover.",
      "seeAlso": [
        "people-s-republic-of-samara",
        "communism"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ptolemy-pryce",
      "term": "Ptolemy Pryce",
      "aliases": [
        "Captain Pryce",
        "Captain Ptolemy Pryce",
        "Pryce",
        "Ptolemaios Pryce"
      ],
      "caseSensitive": true,
      "short": "The captain of Precinct 41, Harry's home station: one of the most respected men in the RCM, glimpsed only through Esprit de Corps visions.",
      "details": "Esprit de Corps shows him as a bald, bespectacled man of 58 who keeps the station's ledger by the light of a green desk lamp and rides home on a piebald horse. Kim says everyone respects the 41st because it has Captain Pryce, and Judit Minot explains that he is the son of the old Pryce, one of the founders of the RCM.\n\nJean Vicquemare has had to explain Harry's behaviour to him more than once.",
      "spoiler": "Jean Vicquemare complains that he has had to explain Harry's behaviour to Pryce more than once. In the closing scene, Pryce tells the precinct medic Nix Gottlieb that Harry is 'our man' and will pull through.",
      "seeAlso": [
        "esprit-de-corps",
        "jean-vicquemare",
        "judit-minot",
        "nix-gottlieb",
        "precinct-41",
        "rcm"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "racist-lorry-driver",
      "term": "Racist lorry driver",
      "short": "A lorry driver stuck in the traffic jam near the harbour, waiting on a load of apples; he greets newcomers with 'Welcome to Revachol!' and has ugly views on race.",
      "details": "He calls himself a camionneur, a father of three and a Revacholian patriot, and boasts of an ancient, privileged guild of lorrymen. Given an audience, he lectures on 'race theory' and the supposed superiority of Occidentals, and he is openly hostile to Kim.\n\nHe complains that cargo is being looted from the stranded lorries at night and points the finger at Siileng, the street vendor down by the canal.",
      "seeAlso": [
        "siileng",
        "kim-kitsuragi",
        "fascism",
        "strike"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "raphael-ambrosius-costeau",
      "term": "Raphaël Ambrosius Costeau",
      "aliases": [
        "Raphaël A. Costeau",
        "Mr. Costeau",
        "Raphael Costeau"
      ],
      "caseSensitive": true,
      "short": "A grand fake name that Conceptualization can invent for you when Kim first asks who you are. You can keep using it around Martinaise.",
      "details": "Having forgotten your real name, you can let Conceptualization dream up something that sounds 'solemn and historical'. Kim plays along with a straight face, the Horrific Necktie loves it, and most people take it with a pinch of salt. Insisting on it can even earn you a Thought.",
      "seeAlso": [
        "harry",
        "conceptualization",
        "tequila-sunset",
        "horrific-necktie"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "rosemary",
      "term": "Rosemary",
      "caseSensitive": true,
      "commonWord": true,
      "short": "A jolly drunk in the fishing village who runs a tiny shop of cigarettes, cheap pilsner, speed and a suspicious bottle of blue 'spirits'.",
      "details": "He hangs out with Idiot Doom Spiral as part of what they call the Union of Moribund Alcoholics, and calls you 'buddy-boy' and 'legend'. He says he used to teach high school biology.\n\nHis prize item is a bottle of blue medicinal spirits, nearly pure alcohol, which he claims to have swiped from a doctor's office (after throwing out the preserved snakes). He keeps forgetting whether it costs 300 réal or 3.",
      "seeAlso": [
        "fishing-village",
        "real",
        "electrochemistry"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ruud-hoenkloewen",
      "term": "Ruud Hoenkloewen",
      "aliases": [
        "Ruud"
      ],
      "caseSensitive": true,
      "hideUntilSeen": true,
      "short": "A silent mercenary in ceramic armour and a full helmet, armed with a long rifle; his comrades call him 'The Killer'.",
      "details": "He barely speaks, and when he does it comes out as a muffled shout from inside his helmet. Little stick figures counted along the rim of the helmet hint at his record.",
      "spoiler": "He is one of the three armed comrades of the dead man who come to Martinaise for revenge and confront you at the tribunal, alongside Kortenaer and De Paule.",
      "seeAlso": [
        "mercenaries",
        "the-hanged-man",
        "scab-leader"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "scab-leader",
      "term": "Scab Leader",
      "caseSensitive": true,
      "short": "The loudest of the strikebreakers outside the harbour gates: a huge, square-jawed man chanting 'Right to work!' at the striking dockworkers.",
      "details": "He claims to be an odd-job man and former bouncer from the Eminent Domain in Jamrock, and he urges the police to open the gates for 'honest workers'. Your skills notice that something is off: his clothes don't fit, his slogans sound memorised, and he tenses up whenever the murder comes up.\n\nAround him, a crowd of desperate scabs shout for work, money and their right to feed their families.",
      "spoiler": "He is a mercenary in disguise, one of the dead man's comrades: Kortenaer, who later reappears in full armour at the tribunal.",
      "seeAlso": [
        "scab",
        "strike",
        "mercenaries",
        "harbour",
        "ruud-hoenkloewen"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "shanky",
      "term": "Shanky",
      "aliases": [
        "Dennis",
        "Shanks"
      ],
      "caseSensitive": true,
      "commonWord": true,
      "short": "A small, rat-faced Hardie Boy with two front teeth missing; he sucks up to Titus and heckles you at every turn.",
      "details": "His real name is Dennis, and his mates also call him Shanks. He is a sneering, cunning man in his forties who sucks up to Titus Hardie and jeers at you whenever you question the gang.\n\nThe other Hardies give it back to him, mostly by teasing him about women and about still living with his mother.",
      "spoiler": "When violence breaks out at the tribunal, Shanky either dies or runs away, depending on how events go; if he runs, the surviving Hardie Boys swear to make him pay.",
      "seeAlso": [
        "hardie-boys",
        "ruby",
        "titus-hardie",
        "whirling-in-rags"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "siileng",
      "term": "Siileng",
      "caseSensitive": true,
      "short": "A relentlessly upbeat street vendor from Siigay who runs an unlicensed stall by the traffic jam, selling clothes, sneakers, sunglasses and food parcels.",
      "details": "Everything at Siileng's is 'cool' and '50% off'. He loves Revachol because no one needs a permit to sell anything, calls himself an independent entrepreneur, and says he sends half his profits to his grandma back home. His stock includes FALN sneakers, sunglasses, a sports visor and some old Samaran speakers he is ashamed of.\n\nThe racist lorry driver nearby accuses him of looting cargo.",
      "spoiler": "Some of his goods are humanitarian aid: food kits marked as not for resale, from the Humanox aid lorry parked nearby, whose security seals have been broken.",
      "seeAlso": [
        "siigay",
        "samara",
        "racist-lorry-driver",
        "ultraliberalism"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "sleeping-dockworker",
      "term": "Sleeping Dockworker",
      "aliases": [
        "Santiago S. John"
      ],
      "caseSensitive": true,
      "short": "A striking dockworker passed out at a table in the Whirling-in-Rags after a bottle or two of rum.",
      "details": "His ID card names him Santiago S. John. With Interfacing you can pick his pocket for a little cash and his dockworker ID card; with enough shouting you can wake him. Awake, he explains the strike, praises Evrart, and warns that the dead man had dangerous friends who might come looking for trouble.",
      "seeAlso": [
        "whirling-in-rags",
        "union",
        "strike",
        "interfacing"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "smoker-on-the-balcony",
      "term": "Smoker on the Balcony",
      "aliases": [
        "Martin Martinaise"
      ],
      "caseSensitive": true,
      "short": "A handsome, evasive young man smoking on a balcony that overlooks the Whirling's backyard; he gives his name as 'Martin Martinaise'.",
      "details": "He talks in a lowered voice, keeps glancing at the neighbouring windows and won't give his real name. He says someone more 'muscular' already asked him about the hanging, and he mentions spending Sunday night with a 'Sunday friend', who might be a witness.\n\nHe lives in apartment #28 of the building, and later turns up in the Whirling-in-Rags as well.",
      "spoiler": "His Sunday friend is a Coalition official who did see something on the night of the hanging. The friend describes him as a young art student whose family emigrated from Kedra.",
      "seeAlso": [
        "sunday-friend",
        "the-hanged-man",
        "whirling-in-rags"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "soona",
      "term": "Soona",
      "aliases": [
        "Soona, the Programmer",
        "Soona Luukanen-Kilde"
      ],
      "caseSensitive": true,
      "short": "A prickly, brilliant programmer who has set up her radiocomputer and strange equipment inside the abandoned church, researching something she won't easily explain.",
      "details": "She is the former lead programmer of Fortress Accident, a failed radio game studio, and of RSA Radios, and calls herself a circuit bender. She has surrounded herself with basins and hydrotransducers, trying to measure a silence in the church and find where it begins.\n\nThe ravers who want the church for a nightclub need her to move, and she will cooperate only if you help her research first, starting with recovering a backup from her old studio in the Doomed Commercial Area.",
      "spoiler": "Her research points to an entroponetic anomaly in the church, which you come to describe as a two-millimetre hole in reality. She can also help you get a radio signal through to Coalition Warship Archer.",
      "seeAlso": [
        "church",
        "radiocomputer",
        "project-dread-board",
        "pale",
        "doomed-commercial-area"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "steban",
      "term": "Steban",
      "aliases": [
        "Steban, the Student Communist"
      ],
      "caseSensitive": true,
      "short": "A young, combative communist student who runs a secret night-time reading group in a disused corner of the Capeside Apartments.",
      "details": "He and his fellow student Ulixes meet only at night, and Steban is paranoid enough to demand passphrases at the door; Cindy the Skull can point you to him. He has expelled most of the group over ideological heresies and loves to argue theory, but underneath he is sincere and gentler than he first seems.\n\nA communist-leaning detective will find plenty to talk about with him.",
      "seeAlso": [
        "communism",
